    detailedAnswer: "**Direct answer:**\n\nCorrelation measures the strength and direction of a statistical association between two variables; it says nothing on its own about whether one causes the other. Two variables can be correlated because one causes the other, because they share a common cause (a confounder), because of reverse causation, or purely by chance, especially when many variable pairs are tested (multiple comparisons). A classic pattern is a confounder driving both ? for example, ice cream sales and drowning incidents both rise in summer because of a shared cause (warm weather), not because ice cream causes drownings. Establishing causation generally requires a randomized experiment, or, when that's not possible, a quasi-experimental design (difference-in-differences, instrumental variables, regression discontinuity) with explicit assumptions about confounding.\n\n**Example:**\n\nA company observes that users who see more push notifications also churn less, and concludes notifications reduce churn. But more engaged users may simply opt into more notifications and were already less likely to churn ? engagement is the confounder, and only a randomized notification experiment could isolate the causal effect.",
