    sampleAnswer: "A frequentist confidence interval is constructed so that, over repeated sampling, a given percentage (say 95%) of such intervals would contain the true parameter value; it is a statement about the long-run behavior of the procedure, not a probability statement about the specific interval you computed containing the true value. A Bayesian credible interval is a direct probability statement: given the data and a prior, there's a 95% probability the parameter lies within that interval. The distinction matters because confidence intervals are frequently (mis)interpreted the Bayesian way in practice, which is technically incorrect under the frequentist framework, even though the numeric intervals are often similar for large samples with weak priors.",
    detailedAnswer: "**Direct answer:**\n\nA frequentist confidence interval is constructed so that, over repeated sampling, a given percentage (say 95%) of such intervals would contain the true parameter value; it is a statement about the long-run behavior of the procedure, not a probability statement about the specific interval you computed containing the true value. A Bayesian credible interval is a direct probability statement: given the data and a prior, there's a 95% probability the parameter lies within that interval. The distinction matters because confidence intervals are frequently (mis)interpreted the Bayesian way in practice, which is technically incorrect under the frequentist framework, even though the numeric intervals are often similar for large samples with weak priors.\n\n**Example:**\n\n\"There's a 95% probability the true conversion rate lies in [4.1%, 4.9%]\" is a Bayesian-style credible-interval statement. The strictly correct frequentist interpretation of a 95% confidence interval is: if we repeated this experiment many times, about 95% of the intervals constructed the same way would contain the true rate.",
