    sampleAnswer: "Mean is the sum of values divided by the count, computable in a single pass. Median requires sorting the values (O(n log n) with Python's Timsort) and then taking the middle element, or averaging the two middle elements for an even-length list. Mode requires counting frequencies, typically with a dictionary, in O(n) time, and then picking the key with the highest count ? being aware that a dataset can have more than one mode (a tie), which a naive max() over the dictionary will silently resolve to just one of them.",
    detailedAnswer: "**Direct answer:**\n\nMean is the sum of values divided by the count, computable in a single pass. Median requires sorting the values (O(n log n) with Python's Timsort) and then taking the middle element, or averaging the two middle elements for an even-length list. Mode requires counting frequencies, typically with a dictionary, in O(n) time, and then picking the key with the highest count ? being aware that a dataset can have more than one mode (a tie), which a naive max() over the dictionary will silently resolve to just one of them.\n\n**Example:**\n\n```python\ndef stats(values):\n    if not values:\n        raise ValueError(\"empty input\")\n    mean = sum(values) / len(values)\n    ordered = sorted(values)\n    n = len(ordered)\n    median = ordered[n // 2] if n % 2 else (ordered[n // 2 - 1] + ordered[n // 2]) / 2\n    counts = {}\n    for x in values:\n        counts[x] = counts.get(x, 0) + 1\n    mode = max(counts, key=counts.get)\n    return mean, median, mode\n```",
