    detailedAnswer: "**Direct answer:**\n\nUse str.split() with expand=True to turn the single column into separate columns in one step, rather than looping row by row, which is both slower and more error-prone. After the split, decide explicitly how to handle rows where the name doesn't have exactly two parts (a single-word name, or a name with a middle name) ? a naive split can silently produce None in the second column or an extra column for a three-part name. Duplicate columns (from a bad merge or accidental re-run of a transformation) should be checked with df.columns.duplicated() and dropped deliberately, not assumed away. Missing values should be handled according to the actual data contract ? dropping, filling with a sentinel, or imputing ? rather than defaulting to one option without justification.\n\n**Example:**\n\n```python\ndf[[\"first_name\", \"last_name\"]] = df[\"full_name\"].str.split(n=1, expand=True)\ndf = df.loc[:, ~df.columns.duplicated()]\ndf[\"last_name\"] = df[\"last_name\"].fillna(\"Unknown\")  # or another explicit missing-value policy\n```",
