    sampleAnswer: "First quantify how much is missing and whether it's missing completely at random, at random conditional on other features, or not at random (e.g., high earners systematically declining to report income) ? the mechanism matters more than the percentage missing when deciding how to handle it. Dropping rows (dropna()) is reasonable when missingness is small and random; dropping a column is reasonable when a feature is missing too often to be useful. Imputing (fillna() with mean/median/mode, or a model-based imputer like KNNImputer or IterativeImputer) preserves sample size but can distort variance and relationships if applied carelessly, especially for data missing not at random. For features feeding a model, adding a binary \"was_missing\" indicator alongside imputed values sometimes preserves useful information that pure imputation would otherwise erase.",
    detailedAnswer: "**Direct answer:**\n\nFirst quantify how much is missing and whether it's missing completely at random, at random conditional on other features, or not at random (e.g., high earners systematically declining to report income) ? the mechanism matters more than the percentage missing when deciding how to handle it. Dropping rows (dropna()) is reasonable when missingness is small and random; dropping a column is reasonable when a feature is missing too often to be useful. Imputing (fillna() with mean/median/mode, or a model-based imputer like KNNImputer or IterativeImputer) preserves sample size but can distort variance and relationships if applied carelessly, especially for data missing not at random. For features feeding a model, adding a binary \"was_missing\" indicator alongside imputed values sometimes preserves useful information that pure imputation would otherwise erase.\n\n**Example:**\n\n```python\ndf[\"income_missing\"] = df[\"income\"].isna().astype(int)\ndf[\"income\"] = df[\"income\"].fillna(df[\"income\"].median())\n# The indicator lets a downstream model learn that missingness itself carried information,\n# rather than treating an imputed median as if it were an observed value.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n\n\nSQL FOR DATA SCIENCE\n\n\n```",
