    sampleAnswer: "The cleanest and most portable modern approach uses DENSE_RANK() so that tied salaries share a rank and don't skip a position (unlike ROW_NUMBER, which would arbitrarily separate ties, or RANK, which would skip subsequent ranks after a tie). An older, more portable-but-fragile approach uses a correlated subquery counting distinct higher salaries, which works on databases without window function support but is harder to read and generalize to \"Nth highest.\" A naive LIMIT/OFFSET approach (ORDER BY salary DESC LIMIT 1 OFFSET 2) is simple but breaks silently when there are duplicate salaries, since it just skips two rows rather than two distinct salary values.",
    detailedAnswer: "**Direct answer:**\n\nThe cleanest and most portable modern approach uses DENSE_RANK() so that tied salaries share a rank and don't skip a position (unlike ROW_NUMBER, which would arbitrarily separate ties, or RANK, which would skip subsequent ranks after a tie). An older, more portable-but-fragile approach uses a correlated subquery counting distinct higher salaries, which works on databases without window function support but is harder to read and generalize to \"Nth highest.\" A naive LIMIT/OFFSET approach (ORDER BY salary DESC LIMIT 1 OFFSET 2) is simple but breaks silently when there are duplicate salaries, since it just skips two rows rather than two distinct salary values.\n\n**Example:**\n\n```sql\nSELECT salary\nFROM (\n    SELECT salary, DENSE_RANK() OVER (ORDER BY salary DESC) AS rnk\n    FROM employees\n) ranked\nWHERE rnk = 3;\n-- DENSE_RANK correctly returns the third-highest DISTINCT salary even with duplicates,\n-- unlike a plain LIMIT 1 OFFSET 2 on ORDER BY salary DESC.\n```",
