    detailedAnswer: "**Direct answer:**\n\nA common real-world pattern: join orders to customers to get a customer's details, but only include customers whose total order value exceeds some threshold ? that threshold check requires an aggregate, so it's expressed either as a subquery in the WHERE/HAVING clause or as a CTE joined back in. Before writing the query, it's worth explicitly stating the grain of each table (one row per order vs. one row per customer) so the join doesn't accidentally fan out rows and inflate later aggregates.\n\n**Example:**\n\n```sql\nSELECT c.customer_id, c.name, o.order_id, o.order_date, o.amount\nFROM customers c\nJOIN orders o ON o.customer_id = c.customer_id\nWHERE c.customer_id IN (\n    SELECT customer_id\n    FROM orders\n    GROUP BY customer_id\n    HAVING SUM(amount) > 10000\n);\n```",
