    sampleAnswer: "Identifying duplicates on a chosen key (or full row) typically uses GROUP BY on that key with HAVING COUNT(*) > 1 to find which keys are duplicated. Safely deleting duplicates while keeping exactly one row per key is best done by ranking rows within each duplicate group (with ROW_NUMBER() OVER PARTITION BY the key) and deleting everything except rank 1, rather than an ad hoc DELETE that could remove every copy including the one you meant to keep. It's worth explicitly deciding which duplicate to keep (e.g., the most recent by a timestamp column) rather than an arbitrary one.",
    detailedAnswer: "**Direct answer:**\n\nIdentifying duplicates on a chosen key (or full row) typically uses GROUP BY on that key with HAVING COUNT(*) > 1 to find which keys are duplicated. Safely deleting duplicates while keeping exactly one row per key is best done by ranking rows within each duplicate group (with ROW_NUMBER() OVER PARTITION BY the key) and deleting everything except rank 1, rather than an ad hoc DELETE that could remove every copy including the one you meant to keep. It's worth explicitly deciding which duplicate to keep (e.g., the most recent by a timestamp column) rather than an arbitrary one.\n\n**Example:**\n\n```sql\nWITH ranked AS (\n    SELECT id, ROW_NUMBER() OVER (PARTITION BY email ORDER BY created_at DESC) AS rn\n    FROM users\n)\nDELETE FROM users WHERE id IN (SELECT id FROM ranked WHERE rn > 1);\n-- Keeps the most recent row per email, deletes the rest.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n\n\nCORE MACHINE LEARNING CONCEPTS\n\n\n```",
