    detailedAnswer: "**Direct answer:**\n\nSupervised learning learns a mapping from features to a known target using labeled examples ? predicting churn, price, or fraud, where historical outcomes are recorded. Unsupervised learning finds structure in data without a target label ? clustering similar customers, reducing dimensionality, or detecting anomalies relative to the bulk of the data. The deciding factor is whether a reliable, sufficiently large set of labeled outcomes actually exists for the problem: if you have historical churn labels, supervised classification is natural; if you're trying to discover customer segments that no one has defined yet, that's inherently unsupervised.\n\n**Example:**\n\nA telecom company with two years of labeled churn/no-churn history for existing customers would build a supervised classifier to predict future churn. The same company, wanting to discover natural customer segments for a new marketing campaign with no predefined segment labels, would use unsupervised clustering (e.g., k-means) instead.",
