    explanation: "Interviewers ask this to see whether you understand how machine-learning methods behave and can choose or evaluate them based on the actual problem.\n\nWhat the interviewer wants:\n- They want the model concept or metric explained precisely.\n- They want you to discuss trade-offs, assumptions, and situations where a method can mislead.\n- They want you to connect model behavior to a concrete business or modeling scenario.",
    sampleAnswer: "Bias is error from a model being too simple to capture the true underlying relationship (systematic underfitting); variance is error from a model being too sensitive to the specific training sample, capturing noise rather than signal (overfitting). Total expected error can be decomposed roughly into bias squared, variance, and irreducible noise. Increasing model complexity (more features, deeper trees, more parameters) typically reduces bias but increases variance, and vice versa, so the goal is finding the complexity level that minimizes total error on unseen data, not minimizing training error alone.",
    detailedAnswer: "**Direct answer:**\n\nBias is error from a model being too simple to capture the true underlying relationship (systematic underfitting); variance is error from a model being too sensitive to the specific training sample, capturing noise rather than signal (overfitting). Total expected error can be decomposed roughly into bias squared, variance, and irreducible noise. Increasing model complexity (more features, deeper trees, more parameters) typically reduces bias but increases variance, and vice versa, so the goal is finding the complexity level that minimizes total error on unseen data, not minimizing training error alone.\n\n**Example:**\n\nA linear regression fit to a clearly nonlinear relationship has high bias ? it systematically misses the pattern regardless of how much data you give it. A very deep, unpruned decision tree fit to a small dataset has high variance ? it fits the training noise almost perfectly but generalizes poorly to new data.",
    keyPoints: ["They want the model concept or metric explained precisely.", "They want you to discuss trade-offs, assumptions, and situations where a method can mislead.", "They want you to connect model behavior to a concrete business or modeling scenario."],
