    sampleAnswer: "Overfitting is when a model learns patterns specific to the training data ? including its noise ? that don't generalize to new data, showing up as much better performance on training data than on held-out validation/test data. It's detected by comparing training versus validation metrics (a large gap is the classic signal) and by using cross-validation rather than a single train/test split, which is more susceptible to a lucky or unlucky split. Prevention includes using a simpler model or fewer features, regularization (L1/L2 penalties, dropout in neural networks), gathering more training data, early stopping during training, and pruning for tree-based models.",
    detailedAnswer: "**Direct answer:**\n\nOverfitting is when a model learns patterns specific to the training data ? including its noise ? that don't generalize to new data, showing up as much better performance on training data than on held-out validation/test data. It's detected by comparing training versus validation metrics (a large gap is the classic signal) and by using cross-validation rather than a single train/test split, which is more susceptible to a lucky or unlucky split. Prevention includes using a simpler model or fewer features, regularization (L1/L2 penalties, dropout in neural networks), gathering more training data, early stopping during training, and pruning for tree-based models.\n\n**Example:**\n\nA model shows 98% accuracy on the training set but 71% on the validation set ? that large gap is a clear overfitting signal, and the fix might be adding L2 regularization, reducing tree depth, or using cross-validation to select hyperparameters instead of tuning against the training set directly.",
