    sampleAnswer: "Precision is the fraction of items predicted positive that are actually positive (TP / (TP + FP)) ? it answers \"when the model says yes, how often is it right?\" Recall is the fraction of actual positives the model successfully identifies (TP / (TP + FN)) ? it answers \"of all the real positives, how many did the model catch?\" There's usually a trade-off between them controlled by the classification threshold. You'd prioritize recall when missing a positive is costly (fraud detection, cancer screening, where a false negative can be severe), and prioritize precision when a false positive is costly or erodes trust (flagging a legitimate transaction as fraud and blocking a customer's card, or a spam filter that misclassifies important email).",
    detailedAnswer: "**Direct answer:**\n\nPrecision is the fraction of items predicted positive that are actually positive (TP / (TP + FP)) ? it answers \"when the model says yes, how often is it right?\" Recall is the fraction of actual positives the model successfully identifies (TP / (TP + FN)) ? it answers \"of all the real positives, how many did the model catch?\" There's usually a trade-off between them controlled by the classification threshold. You'd prioritize recall when missing a positive is costly (fraud detection, cancer screening, where a false negative can be severe), and prioritize precision when a false positive is costly or erodes trust (flagging a legitimate transaction as fraud and blocking a customer's card, or a spam filter that misclassifies important email).\n\n**Example:**\n\nA fraud-detection model at a bank is tuned for high recall ? even at the cost of more false positives requiring manual review ? because letting fraudulent transactions through (false negatives) is far more expensive than the operational cost of reviewing some legitimate transactions flagged incorrectly.",
