    shortDescription: "ROC-AUC plots true positive rate against false positive rate across all classification thresholds and summarizes the curve as a single number between 0.5 (no better than random) an...",
    explanation: "Interviewers ask this to see whether you understand how machine-learning methods behave and can choose or evaluate them based on the actual problem.\n\nWhat the interviewer wants:\n- They want the model concept or metric explained precisely.\n- They want you to discuss trade-offs, assumptions, and situations where a method can mislead.\n- They want you to connect model behavior to a concrete business or modeling scenario.",
    sampleAnswer: "ROC-AUC plots true positive rate against false positive rate across all classification thresholds and summarizes the curve as a single number between 0.5 (no better than random) and 1 (perfect separation); it measures how well the model ranks positives above negatives, independent of any single threshold. It's a poor choice, or at least an incomplete one, when classes are heavily imbalanced, because the false positive rate denominator (true negatives, which are abundant in an imbalanced dataset) can make the ROC curve look deceptively good even when precision on the minority class is poor. In that setting, a Precision-Recall AUC curve is usually more informative since it focuses specifically on how well the model performs on the positive (often minority) class.",
    detailedAnswer: "**Direct answer:**\n\nROC-AUC plots true positive rate against false positive rate across all classification thresholds and summarizes the curve as a single number between 0.5 (no better than random) and 1 (perfect separation); it measures how well the model ranks positives above negatives, independent of any single threshold. It's a poor choice, or at least an incomplete one, when classes are heavily imbalanced, because the false positive rate denominator (true negatives, which are abundant in an imbalanced dataset) can make the ROC curve look deceptively good even when precision on the minority class is poor. In that setting, a Precision-Recall AUC curve is usually more informative since it focuses specifically on how well the model performs on the positive (often minority) class.\n\n**Example:**\n\nFor a fraud dataset where only 0.5% of transactions are fraudulent, a model could achieve a high ROC-AUC while still having poor precision at any threshold useful in practice ? checking the PR-AUC and the actual precision/recall at the chosen operating threshold gives a more honest picture.",
