    shortDescription: "K-fold cross-validation splits the data into k roughly equal folds, trains the model k times, each time holding out one fold for validation and training on the remaining k-1 folds,...",
    explanation: "Interviewers ask this to see whether you understand how machine-learning methods behave and can choose or evaluate them based on the actual problem.\n\nWhat the interviewer wants:\n- They want the model concept or metric explained precisely.\n- They want you to discuss trade-offs, assumptions, and situations where a method can mislead.\n- They want you to connect model behavior to a concrete business or modeling scenario.",
    sampleAnswer: "K-fold cross-validation splits the data into k roughly equal folds, trains the model k times, each time holding out one fold for validation and training on the remaining k-1 folds, then averages the performance metric across the k runs. This is generally preferred over a single train/test split because a single split's performance estimate can be noisy ? it depends heavily on which specific rows happened to land in the test set ? whereas averaging over k different splits gives a more stable estimate of how the model is likely to perform on unseen data, and also gives a sense of the variance of that estimate across folds. For time-series data specifically, ordinary k-fold is inappropriate because it can train on future data to predict the past; a time-based split (or expanding-window/forward-chaining cross-validation) should be used instead to respect temporal order.",
    detailedAnswer: "**Direct answer:**\n\nK-fold cross-validation splits the data into k roughly equal folds, trains the model k times, each time holding out one fold for validation and training on the remaining k-1 folds, then averages the performance metric across the k runs. This is generally preferred over a single train/test split because a single split's performance estimate can be noisy ? it depends heavily on which specific rows happened to land in the test set ? whereas averaging over k different splits gives a more stable estimate of how the model is likely to perform on unseen data, and also gives a sense of the variance of that estimate across folds. For time-series data specifically, ordinary k-fold is inappropriate because it can train on future data to predict the past; a time-based split (or expanding-window/forward-chaining cross-validation) should be used instead to respect temporal order.\n\n**Example:**\n\nA 5-fold cross-validation on a churn model trains on 80% of the data and validates on the remaining 20%, five times with a different 20% held out each time, then reports the mean and standard deviation of AUC across the five folds ? giving a more reliable estimate than a single 80/20 split.",
