    shortDescription: "Regularization adds a penalty term to a model's loss function based on the magnitude of its coefficients, discouraging overly large weights and reducing overfitting/variance at the...",
    explanation: "Interviewers ask this to see whether you understand how machine-learning methods behave and can choose or evaluate them based on the actual problem.\n\nWhat the interviewer wants:\n- They want the model concept or metric explained precisely.\n- They want you to discuss trade-offs, assumptions, and situations where a method can mislead.\n- They want you to connect model behavior to a concrete business or modeling scenario.",
    sampleAnswer: "Regularization adds a penalty term to a model's loss function based on the magnitude of its coefficients, discouraging overly large weights and reducing overfitting/variance at the cost of some added bias. L1 (Lasso) penalizes the sum of absolute coefficient values, and its geometry tends to push some coefficients exactly to zero, which effectively performs feature selection ? useful when you suspect many features are irrelevant. L2 (Ridge) penalizes the sum of squared coefficients, shrinking all coefficients toward zero smoothly without necessarily eliminating any, which tends to work better when most features carry some real signal and features are correlated (L2 handles multicollinearity more gracefully than L1). Elastic Net combines both penalties when you want some feature selection alongside the stability L2 provides.",
    detailedAnswer: "**Direct answer:**\n\nRegularization adds a penalty term to a model's loss function based on the magnitude of its coefficients, discouraging overly large weights and reducing overfitting/variance at the cost of some added bias. L1 (Lasso) penalizes the sum of absolute coefficient values, and its geometry tends to push some coefficients exactly to zero, which effectively performs feature selection ? useful when you suspect many features are irrelevant. L2 (Ridge) penalizes the sum of squared coefficients, shrinking all coefficients toward zero smoothly without necessarily eliminating any, which tends to work better when most features carry some real signal and features are correlated (L2 handles multicollinearity more gracefully than L1). Elastic Net combines both penalties when you want some feature selection alongside the stability L2 provides.\n\n**Example:**\n\nA model with 500 candidate features, most of which are likely noise, would benefit from L1 regularization to automatically zero out irrelevant coefficients and produce a sparser, more interpretable model, whereas a model with a smaller set of features that are all plausibly relevant and correlated would typically do better with L2.",
