    shortDescription: "A Random Forest reduces variance by training many decision trees on bootstrapped samples of the data (bagging) with additional randomness from selecting a random subset of features...",
    explanation: "Interviewers ask this to see whether you understand how machine-learning methods behave and can choose or evaluate them based on the actual problem.\n\nWhat the interviewer wants:\n- They want the model concept or metric explained precisely.\n- They want you to discuss trade-offs, assumptions, and situations where a method can mislead.\n- They want you to connect model behavior to a concrete business or modeling scenario.",
    sampleAnswer: "A Random Forest reduces variance by training many decision trees on bootstrapped samples of the data (bagging) with additional randomness from selecting a random subset of features at each split, then averaging their predictions (or voting, for classification). Because the trees are decorrelated from each other, averaging cancels out much of each individual tree's overfitting to its own bootstrap sample, reducing overall variance while keeping bias roughly similar to a single deep tree. Boosting (AdaBoost, Gradient Boosting) works differently ? it builds trees sequentially, with each new tree specifically targeting the errors of the previous ensemble, which primarily reduces bias by incrementally correcting systematic mistakes, though it can increase variance if run for too many rounds without proper regularization (learning rate, max depth, early stopping).",
    detailedAnswer: "**Direct answer:**\n\nA Random Forest reduces variance by training many decision trees on bootstrapped samples of the data (bagging) with additional randomness from selecting a random subset of features at each split, then averaging their predictions (or voting, for classification). Because the trees are decorrelated from each other, averaging cancels out much of each individual tree's overfitting to its own bootstrap sample, reducing overall variance while keeping bias roughly similar to a single deep tree. Boosting (AdaBoost, Gradient Boosting) works differently ? it builds trees sequentially, with each new tree specifically targeting the errors of the previous ensemble, which primarily reduces bias by incrementally correcting systematic mistakes, though it can increase variance if run for too many rounds without proper regularization (learning rate, max depth, early stopping).\n\n**Example:**\n\nA single deep decision tree overfits the training data (high variance). A Random Forest of 200 such trees, each trained on a different bootstrap sample with random feature subsets, averages out much of that individual overfitting and generalizes better, without needing to make any single tree simpler.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n\n\nFEATURE ENGINEERING & DATA PREPARATION\n\n",
