    interviewType: "TECHNICAL",
    shortDescription: "Data leakage happens when information that wouldn't be available at actual prediction time is inadvertently used during training, making the model look far more accurate in validat...",
    explanation: "Interviewers ask this because data preparation decisions can materially change model validity, generalization, and production behavior.\n\nWhat the interviewer wants:\n- They want you to identify the source of leakage, bias, or distorted feature behavior.\n- They want a preprocessing strategy that respects the training/validation boundary.\n- They want you to explain when different preprocessing choices are appropriate.",
    sampleAnswer: "Data leakage happens when information that wouldn't be available at actual prediction time is inadvertently used during training, making the model look far more accurate in validation than it will be in production. Common causes include: fitting a scaler, imputer, or feature-selection step on the full dataset before splitting into train/test (leaking test-set statistics into training); including a feature that's a proxy for or directly derived from the target (like using 'total refunds issued' to predict 'is this a fraudulent order' when refunds only happen after the fraud investigation concludes); and, in time-series problems, using future information to predict the past because of an improper (non-time-aware) train/test split.",
    detailedAnswer: "**Direct answer:**\n\nData leakage happens when information that wouldn't be available at actual prediction time is inadvertently used during training, making the model look far more accurate in validation than it will be in production. Common causes include: fitting a scaler, imputer, or feature-selection step on the full dataset before splitting into train/test (leaking test-set statistics into training); including a feature that's a proxy for or directly derived from the target (like using 'total refunds issued' to predict 'is this a fraudulent order' when refunds only happen after the fraud investigation concludes); and, in time-series problems, using future information to predict the past because of an improper (non-time-aware) train/test split.\n\n**Example:**\n\nA churn model accidentally includes a 'days since last login' feature that's computed using data up through the current date, even for customers who already churned months ago ? meaning the feature indirectly encodes the outcome itself. Fixing it requires computing every feature strictly as of the prediction date, not the date the dataset was pulled.",
    keyPoints: ["They want you to identify the source of leakage, bias, or distorted feature behavior.", "They want a preprocessing strategy that respects the training/validation boundary.", "They want you to explain when different preprocessing choices are appropriate."],
