    sampleAnswer: "One-hot encoding a high-cardinality column creates thousands of mostly-sparse columns, which bloats memory and can hurt tree-based models by diluting the signal across many rare categories. Better options depend on the model type: for tree-based models, target encoding (replacing each category with a statistic like the mean target value for that category, computed carefully with cross-validation or smoothing to avoid leakage) or frequency encoding often works well; for linear or neural models, learned embeddings (similar to word embeddings) can capture relationships between categories in a lower-dimensional space. Grouping rare categories into an \"other\" bucket is also a simple, effective baseline when many categories individually have very few observations.",
    detailedAnswer: "**Direct answer:**\n\nOne-hot encoding a high-cardinality column creates thousands of mostly-sparse columns, which bloats memory and can hurt tree-based models by diluting the signal across many rare categories. Better options depend on the model type: for tree-based models, target encoding (replacing each category with a statistic like the mean target value for that category, computed carefully with cross-validation or smoothing to avoid leakage) or frequency encoding often works well; for linear or neural models, learned embeddings (similar to word embeddings) can capture relationships between categories in a lower-dimensional space. Grouping rare categories into an \"other\" bucket is also a simple, effective baseline when many categories individually have very few observations.\n\n**Example:**\n\nA 'merchant_id' feature with 50,000 unique values would overwhelm a one-hot encoding. Target-encoding each merchant by its historical average fraud rate (computed only from training folds to avoid leakage) gives the model a compact, informative numeric feature instead.",
