    sampleAnswer: "Feature scaling transforms numeric features onto a comparable range or distribution, commonly standardization (subtract mean, divide by standard deviation) or min-max normalization. Models that are distance-based or gradient-based generally need it: k-nearest neighbors and k-means (since raw distance calculations would be dominated by whichever feature has the largest numeric range), support vector machines, linear/logistic regression trained with gradient descent (unscaled features can make optimization slow or unstable), and neural networks. Tree-based models (decision trees, Random Forest, gradient boosting) generally don't need feature scaling, since splits are based on relative ordering within a feature, not on distances or gradients across features.",
    detailedAnswer: "**Direct answer:**\n\nFeature scaling transforms numeric features onto a comparable range or distribution, commonly standardization (subtract mean, divide by standard deviation) or min-max normalization. Models that are distance-based or gradient-based generally need it: k-nearest neighbors and k-means (since raw distance calculations would be dominated by whichever feature has the largest numeric range), support vector machines, linear/logistic regression trained with gradient descent (unscaled features can make optimization slow or unstable), and neural networks. Tree-based models (decision trees, Random Forest, gradient boosting) generally don't need feature scaling, since splits are based on relative ordering within a feature, not on distances or gradients across features.\n\n**Example:**\n\nA dataset with 'age' (roughly 18-90) and 'annual income' (roughly 20,000-500,000) fed into k-nearest neighbors without scaling would let income differences completely dominate the distance calculation, effectively ignoring age ? standardizing both features first fixes that.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n\n\nDEEP LEARNING BASICS\n\n",
