    explanation: "Interviewers ask this to test whether you understand the mechanics and practical limitations of neural-network training rather than only knowing model names.\n\nWhat the interviewer wants:\n- They want the learning mechanism explained from inputs through loss and parameter updates.\n- They want you to identify the training problem or architectural trade-off involved.\n- They want a practical example showing how the concept affects model training or model selection.",
    sampleAnswer: "A neural network makes a prediction via a forward pass through its layers, a loss function quantifies how wrong that prediction was compared to the true target, and backpropagation uses the chain rule to compute the gradient of the loss with respect to every weight in the network ? effectively, how much each weight contributed to the error. An optimizer (like SGD or Adam) then updates each weight in the direction that reduces the loss, scaled by the learning rate, which controls how large each update step is. Too high a learning rate can cause training to diverge or oscillate; too low a learning rate makes training very slow and can get stuck in poor local regions. This forward-pass/backward-pass/update cycle repeats over many batches and epochs until the loss on a validation set stops improving.",
    detailedAnswer: "**Direct answer:**\n\nA neural network makes a prediction via a forward pass through its layers, a loss function quantifies how wrong that prediction was compared to the true target, and backpropagation uses the chain rule to compute the gradient of the loss with respect to every weight in the network ? effectively, how much each weight contributed to the error. An optimizer (like SGD or Adam) then updates each weight in the direction that reduces the loss, scaled by the learning rate, which controls how large each update step is. Too high a learning rate can cause training to diverge or oscillate; too low a learning rate makes training very slow and can get stuck in poor local regions. This forward-pass/backward-pass/update cycle repeats over many batches and epochs until the loss on a validation set stops improving.\n\n**Example:**\n\nTraining an image classifier: a batch of images goes through the network (forward pass), cross-entropy loss compares predicted class probabilities to true labels, backpropagation computes each weight's gradient, and Adam nudges every weight slightly to reduce that batch's loss ? repeated over thousands of batches.",
