    sampleAnswer: "For structured/tabular data with a moderate number of features and rows (thousands to a few million), gradient-boosted trees are usually the practical default ? they tend to match or beat deep learning on tabular data, train faster, need less data and less hyperparameter tuning, and are easier to interpret with tools like SHAP. Deep learning tends to pull ahead when the data is unstructured (images, audio, raw text, video) where it can learn hierarchical feature representations that would otherwise require extensive manual feature engineering, or when the dataset is very large and complex relationships benefit from the extra model capacity. The decision should also weigh available compute, latency requirements at inference time, and how much labeled data actually exists, since deep learning generally needs substantially more data to reach its advantage over simpler methods.",
    detailedAnswer: "**Direct answer:**\n\nFor structured/tabular data with a moderate number of features and rows (thousands to a few million), gradient-boosted trees are usually the practical default ? they tend to match or beat deep learning on tabular data, train faster, need less data and less hyperparameter tuning, and are easier to interpret with tools like SHAP. Deep learning tends to pull ahead when the data is unstructured (images, audio, raw text, video) where it can learn hierarchical feature representations that would otherwise require extensive manual feature engineering, or when the dataset is very large and complex relationships benefit from the extra model capacity. The decision should also weigh available compute, latency requirements at inference time, and how much labeled data actually exists, since deep learning generally needs substantially more data to reach its advantage over simpler methods.\n\n**Example:**\n\nA tabular customer-churn dataset with 200,000 rows and 40 features would typically be solved effectively and quickly with LightGBM. A product-image-classification problem with millions of labeled photos would call for a convolutional or transformer-based vision model instead, since tree-based methods can't directly exploit raw pixel structure.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n\n\n\n\n",
