    sampleAnswer: "Before launching, define a single primary metric tied directly to the business question (not several competing metrics chosen after the fact), the minimum detectable effect worth caring about, and use a power analysis to determine the required sample size and expected test duration given that effect size and baseline metric variance. Decide the randomization unit (user, session, device) matching how the effect is expected to manifest, and confirm there's no contamination between groups (e.g., a shared household seeing both variants). Pre-register the analysis plan, including the significance threshold and any guardrail metrics that must not regress even if the primary metric improves, to avoid the temptation to change the analysis after seeing early results.",
    detailedAnswer: "**Direct answer:**\n\nBefore launching, define a single primary metric tied directly to the business question (not several competing metrics chosen after the fact), the minimum detectable effect worth caring about, and use a power analysis to determine the required sample size and expected test duration given that effect size and baseline metric variance. Decide the randomization unit (user, session, device) matching how the effect is expected to manifest, and confirm there's no contamination between groups (e.g., a shared household seeing both variants). Pre-register the analysis plan, including the significance threshold and any guardrail metrics that must not regress even if the primary metric improves, to avoid the temptation to change the analysis after seeing early results.\n\n**Example:**\n\nTesting a new checkout flow: primary metric is completed-purchase rate per session; minimum detectable effect set at a 1 percentage point lift based on business relevance; a power analysis determines roughly 40,000 sessions per arm are needed at 80% power; randomization is done at the session level; and a guardrail metric (average order value) is tracked to catch an unintended regression even if conversion improves.",
    keyPoints: ["They want the experimental design and statistical issue explained clearly.", "They want you to identify sources of bias, false positives, or misleading aggregation.", "They want a concrete example of how the issue could change a product decision."],
