    subcategorySlug: "experimentation-and-ab-testing",
    experienceLevel: "EXPERIENCED",
    difficulty: "HARD",
    interviewType: "TECHNICAL",
    shortDescription: "\"Peeking\" means repeatedly checking a test's p-value as data accumulates and stopping the test as soon as it crosses significance, rather than waiting for the pre-determined sample...",
    explanation: "Interviewers ask this to determine whether you can design experiments that produce trustworthy evidence instead of conclusions driven by statistical artifacts.\n\nWhat the interviewer wants:\n- They want the experimental design and statistical issue explained clearly.\n- They want you to identify sources of bias, false positives, or misleading aggregation.\n- They want a concrete example of how the issue could change a product decision.",
    sampleAnswer: "\"Peeking\" means repeatedly checking a test's p-value as data accumulates and stopping the test as soon as it crosses significance, rather than waiting for the pre-determined sample size. Because a p-value fluctuates randomly around the null distribution even when there's truly no effect, checking it many times and stopping at the first moment it dips below 0.05 gives that random fluctuation many chances to cross the threshold by pure chance ? inflating the actual false-positive rate well above the nominal 5%, even though each individual check looks statistically valid in isolation. The fix is either committing to a fixed sample size determined in advance (classical fixed-horizon testing) or using a sequential testing methodology (like alpha-spending or Bayesian sequential methods) explicitly designed to allow valid early stopping.",
    detailedAnswer: "**Direct answer:**\n\n\"Peeking\" means repeatedly checking a test's p-value as data accumulates and stopping the test as soon as it crosses significance, rather than waiting for the pre-determined sample size. Because a p-value fluctuates randomly around the null distribution even when there's truly no effect, checking it many times and stopping at the first moment it dips below 0.05 gives that random fluctuation many chances to cross the threshold by pure chance ? inflating the actual false-positive rate well above the nominal 5%, even though each individual check looks statistically valid in isolation. The fix is either committing to a fixed sample size determined in advance (classical fixed-horizon testing) or using a sequential testing methodology (like alpha-spending or Bayesian sequential methods) explicitly designed to allow valid early stopping.\n\n**Example:**\n\nA test genuinely has no real effect, but the p-value is checked daily over three weeks. Pure random noise means the p-value will very likely dip below 0.05 on at least one of those checks, purely by chance ? stopping and declaring a win at that moment produces a false positive far more often than 5% of the time.",
    keyPoints: ["They want the experimental design and statistical issue explained clearly.", "They want you to identify sources of bias, false positives, or misleading aggregation.", "They want a concrete example of how the issue could change a product decision."],
