    shortDescription: "Start by clarifying the business objective with the stakeholder: what action will actually be taken based on the model's predictions (a retention offer, an outreach call), since th...",
    explanation: "Interviewers ask this to assess whether you can connect technical data-science work to a measurable business objective and make defensible modeling decisions.\n\nWhat the interviewer wants:\n- They want you to start from the business objective, data constraints, and success metric.\n- They want you to justify technical choices and acknowledge reasonable alternatives.\n- They want a practical path from analysis or modeling to validation and measurable impact.",
    sampleAnswer: "Start by clarifying the business objective with the stakeholder: what action will actually be taken based on the model's predictions (a retention offer, an outreach call), since that determines what \"churn\" means (a specific definition and time window) and what error trade-off matters (false negatives, missing a customer who will churn, versus false positives, wasting a retention offer on someone who wouldn't have churned anyway). Define the prediction grain and the exact label window (e.g., \"will this customer churn in the next 30 days\"), assemble features using only information available as of the prediction date to avoid leakage, and choose an evaluation metric aligned to the business cost (often precision/recall or expected value at a chosen threshold rather than plain accuracy, since churn is usually imbalanced). Build a simple baseline first (logistic regression or a basic tree-based model) before reaching for something more complex, validate with a properly time-aware split if the data has a temporal component, and finally connect the model's output back to an actionable threshold and a plan for measuring the actual business impact of acting on its predictions, ideally validated with a holdout or randomized rollout rather than assumed from offline metrics alone.",
    detailedAnswer: "**Direct answer:**\n\nStart by clarifying the business objective with the stakeholder: what action will actually be taken based on the model's predictions (a retention offer, an outreach call), since that determines what \"churn\" means (a specific definition and time window) and what error trade-off matters (false negatives, missing a customer who will churn, versus false positives, wasting a retention offer on someone who wouldn't have churned anyway). Define the prediction grain and the exact label window (e.g., \"will this customer churn in the next 30 days\"), assemble features using only information available as of the prediction date to avoid leakage, and choose an evaluation metric aligned to the business cost (often precision/recall or expected value at a chosen threshold rather than plain accuracy, since churn is usually imbalanced). Build a simple baseline first (logistic regression or a basic tree-based model) before reaching for something more complex, validate with a properly time-aware split if the data has a temporal component, and finally connect the model's output back to an actionable threshold and a plan for measuring the actual business impact of acting on its predictions, ideally validated with a holdout or randomized rollout rather than assumed from offline metrics alone.\n\n**Example:**\n\nRather than jumping straight to a gradient-boosted model, a first pass with logistic regression on a handful of well-understood features (tenure, recent usage trend, support-ticket count) gives a fast, interpretable baseline and a sanity check on the label definition and feature pipeline before investing in a more complex model.",
