    detailedAnswer: "**Direct answer:**\n\nStructure it around the actual decision points, not just the final architecture: what was the business problem and how was success measured, what data was available and what were its limitations, what approaches were considered and why one was chosen over the alternatives, what went wrong or had to be iterated on, and what the measured outcome was once deployed (or why it wasn't deployed, if that's the honest answer). Interviewers probe project descriptions specifically to separate genuine ownership from a rehearsed summary, so being ready to go one level deeper on any single decision ? why that evaluation metric, why that specific feature, what happened when the model was retrained ? matters more than the polish of the initial summary.\n\n**Example:**\n\nRather than \"I built a recommendation system using collaborative filtering,\" a stronger walkthrough covers: the cold-start problem for new users that pure collaborative filtering couldn't handle, why a hybrid approach blending content-based features was added, how offline NDCG was validated against an eventual small online A/B test, and what metric actually moved (or didn't) after launch.",
