    difficulty: "MEDIUM",
    interviewType: "TECHNICAL",
    shortDescription: "A decision tree recursively partitions the training data into subsets that are progressively more homogeneous with respect to the target. For classification, a common split-quality...",
    explanation: "Interviewers ask this to verify that you understand the underlying data-science concept and can apply it correctly to a realistic problem.\n\nWhat the interviewer wants:\n- They want a precise explanation of the core idea.\n- They want you to identify the important assumption, trade-off, or edge case.\n- They want a practical example showing how you would use the concept.",
    sampleAnswer: "A decision tree recursively partitions the training data into subsets that are progressively more homogeneous with respect to the target. For classification, a common split-quality criterion is Gini impurity or entropy; for regression, variance reduction. Building it from scratch requires: evaluating candidate features and thresholds, scoring each candidate split by the weighted impurity of the resulting child nodes, picking the best split, recursing on each child, and stopping based on a condition like maximum depth, minimum samples per leaf, or no further impurity reduction. The computationally expensive part is evaluating many feature/threshold combinations at each node, which real implementations optimize with sorting and incremental impurity updates rather than recomputing from scratch for every candidate threshold.",
    detailedAnswer: "**Direct answer:**\n\nA decision tree recursively partitions the training data into subsets that are progressively more homogeneous with respect to the target. For classification, a common split-quality criterion is Gini impurity or entropy; for regression, variance reduction. Building it from scratch requires: evaluating candidate features and thresholds, scoring each candidate split by the weighted impurity of the resulting child nodes, picking the best split, recursing on each child, and stopping based on a condition like maximum depth, minimum samples per leaf, or no further impurity reduction. The computationally expensive part is evaluating many feature/threshold combinations at each node, which real implementations optimize with sorting and incremental impurity updates rather than recomputing from scratch for every candidate threshold.\n\n**Example:**\n\n```python\ndef gini(labels):\n    n = len(labels)\n    counts = {}\n    for y in labels:\n        counts[y] = counts.get(y, 0) + 1\n    return 1 - sum((c / n) ** 2 for c in counts.values())\n\n# A full implementation enumerates candidate feature thresholds, computes the\n# weighted Gini of the resulting left/right children for each, picks the split\n# with the lowest weighted impurity, and recurses until a stopping condition is met.\n```",
